No, the CD-ROM is set to ID2 and the Seagate drive was 0 -the NCR 53C810
integrated SCSI bus is ID 7.

Self propagating CD entries is not the only weirdness either, the floppy
drive won't function, it's constantly lit up.

I found you can remove the "CDAn" entries individually with the clear
command, and there is a clear_srm_nvram command too (though it doesn't
affect the unwanted entries.

I cannot find a comprehensive index of SRM variables and I don't know what
they are doing. I have also found that some are protected from the clear
command resetting them.

One last thing -the kernel panic caused by failure to mount the root file
system is not helped by declaring any of the boot flags I have tried for the
root file system and I got a message:-

"request module [block-major-8]  Root fs not mounted"

