On Tue, Apr 01, 2003 at 11:07:33PM +0100, Simon Tierney wrote:
> Does anyone know why my digital 7305 is now failing to mount the root
> filesystem and going into kernel panic with various error messages
> including:-
> 
> "you didn't specify the type of your ufs filesystem"

If you are indeed using ufs as a file system then you have to
specify its type.  See 'Documentation/filesystems/ufs.txt' from
kernel documentation for details.

> I have a Debian 3 Woody distro for alpha in the CD-ROM drive and I am
> booting from that, although the srm boot variables point to the Seagate SCSI
> drive with ext2 filesystem and RedHat 7.2 installed with anaconda errors.

If this was not supposed to be ufs then it sounds like you have
some corruption on file systems superblock(s) so they get misdetected.
Or if this was a "recycled" disk then maybe some garbage was left
on it which is confusing a file system detection.  Possibly adding
to your kernel boot parameters something like "rootfstype=ext2",
or "...=ext3", may help.  If your disk is truly messed up this
can be hard to recover.  Booting into a ramdisk, with enough
tools on it, may help to examine what is really going on.

  Michal




