Well,

The AS2100A is finally supported by Alpha Linux!!!!

I am able to boot the new kernel on my Lynx box, but I can't manage to 
re-work the installation CD-ROMS to load the os on the system.

I have tried making the iso's to support the new distribution.  I 
started with the June2001 7.2 release CD's,  copying in new versions of 
the RPMS, rebuilding the boot disk (adding new kernel) and driver disks 
(adding correct version of modules using cpio) after mounting in 
loopback mode.  Nevertheless, I cannot make a booting iso using mkisofs 
(tried using generic.img as an elTorito boot file and some other 
things).  When I boot from floppies (this works), I cannot load drivers 
from the driver disk to mount the CD and start loading RPMS.

I am sure that the problem with making a boot disk is related to loading 
the boot block on the file system, but I can't find any documentation on 
how to do it for alpha.  I suspect that the problems in loading the 
driver modules lie in the versions of modutils in ramdisk.img. 
 Tomorrow, I will try to add new versions of all files in the ramdisk, 
based on an updated alpha file tree on one of my XP-1000's.

I have been using alpha linux for about ten years now, and I would like 
to get it running on these three quad AS2100A's so we can use these 
platforms in our research work (as a cluster), but I am spending an 
awful lot of time trying to get it installed on a test system.

My goal is to install it, and begin testing of the changes George and 
Jay have put in the kernel source.  I fully understand how thankless it 
for them to spend time supporting such an old platform, and they are 
tireless advocates for the cause of AlphaLinux.  However old they may 
be, these Lynx boxes are really quite good for computational stuff, and 
I want to take advantage of these systems for our numerical analysis 
studies, and as a large-scale platform for students to learn how to use.

If anyone can point me in the right direction to create a usable set of 
CD-ROM's for the Alpha platform, I would really appreciate it!






