On Thu, Apr 03, 2003 at 08:07:32PM -0600, Michael A. Mackey wrote:
> 
> I have tried making the iso's to support the new distribution.  I 
> started with the June2001 7.2 release CD's,  copying in new versions of 
> the RPMS,

Once you will do that you have to run 'genhdlist' from
'anaconda-runtime' package.  Otherwise installer will be searching
for packages you just replaced and, surprisingly enough, the
whole process will just die.

> Nevertheless, I cannot make a booting iso using mkisofs 
> (tried using generic.img as an elTorito boot file and some other 
> things).

"elTorito" is for booting CDs with a help of BIOS you will find
on x86 machines of a PC kind.  As your firmware is of a quite
different type there is not so amazing that this fails.

In 'aboot' package there is an 'isomarkboot' utility which modifies iso
images in such way that they become SRM-bootable.  Write such file,
mount it over a loop device and use 'isomarkboot'.  You need to supply a
boot block image which is also part of 'aboot'.  "Native" 'isomarkboot'
binaries are for Alpha but it is possible to hack sources a bit,
recompile and run that on any other Linux machine.

> When I boot from floppies (this works), I cannot load drivers 
> from the driver disk to mount the CD and start loading RPMS.

It should be possible to boot from a floppy but load a sizeable
initrd image from a CD.  Some source code modifications are likely
needed but, at least with ATAPI CD ROMS, this is not that hard.
Making a bootable CD is still likely quite easier. :-)

  Michal




