It appears that you are unfamiliar with RH7.2 & ALPHA.
  The standard for gcc & g++ compilers on this last ( ie RH is not 
making any more  ALPHA iso's )  release:
1) gcc version 2.96 20000731 (Red Hat Linux 7.1 2.96-102)
2) gcc version 2.96 20000731 (Red Hat Linux 7.1 2.96-102) ( very 
interesting for g++ -v )

g++3 yields:
1) gcc version 3.0.4 (Red Hat Linux 7.2 3.0.4-1)

One has to manually ( as I did ) install the g++3 compiler in order to 
begin building the OO src. My impression is that the g++3 is not a 
really supported product for the RH7.2/ALPHA release ( ergo its somewhat 
experimental ). This also goes with the gcc3 compiler. You take your 
poison -- I gotta take g++3, its not necessarily so with the "C" component.
Time will tell if I can present a mix of gcc & g++3 objects to the 
linker ( whats another day in the life of this build ;-} ). I might 
agree if the mix was with g++ & g++3 that these objects will cause no 
end of grief, but I'd still like to believe that "C" is still "simple & 
stupid" enough to mix gcc & gcc3 at whim.


