On Friday 04 April 2003 07:12 pm, U. George wrote:
> Time will
> tell if I can present a mix of gcc & g++3 objects to the
> linker ( whats another day in the life of this build ;-} ). I
> might agree if the mix was with g++ & g++3 that these objects
> will cause no end of grief, but I'd still like to believe that
> "C" is still "simple & stupid" enough to mix gcc & gcc3 at
> whim.

I would say not.  I've already seen that mixing gcc2 and gcc3 for 
kernels and kernel modules leads to rapid oopses.  I'd suspect 
trying to link mixed relocatables from gcc2 and gcc3 would be 
problematic in userland as well.

I _do_ know that recent binutils (say 2.13.x) can function as a 
linker backend to both gcc2 and gcc3.  Mixing binaries and 
shared objects is also not a problem, as long as the libstdc++ 
ABI doesn't get dragged into it--gcc3 apparently just made 
things incompatible up to the relocatable level.

-- 
