On Saturday 05 April 2003 04:36 am, U. George wrote:
> Ahh, but do u know why they are incompatable? Maybe a compiler
> defect?

Well, gcc 3.x (specifically, somewhere around gcc-3.1.1) started 
using some new relocatable symbol types on Alpha (17 and 18, 
R_ALPHA_GPRELHIGH and R_ALPHA_GPRELLOW respectively).  There are 
probably a few other changes as well (not just Alpha-specific) 
due to new features, rather than compiler defects.

> Anyway, if this were true, then there is no real need to
> install the gcc3 ( et al ) product on the ALPHA. Unless, or
> course you remove all things built with gcc2, which in case of
> this RH7.2/ALPHA release whould just about mean everything. Or
> somehow have fenced off areas, one for all things built with
> gcc2, all other things built with gcc3.

Well, as I mentioned, binaries and shared libraries produced by 
gcc2 or gcc3 can be mixed without problems, as long as libstdc++ 
doesn't enter into the picture.  That's pretty much all that 
matters at run-time, except for rare packages like XFree86 and 
the kernel that load .o files at run-time.

The deal-breaker is libstdc++.  This library represents the C++ 
ABI, which didn't really approach finalization until gcc-3.2.  
gcc 2.x had a pretty consistent C++ ABI throughout its life, but 
it was based on the incomplete draft C++ standard of its time, 
rather than the finalized ANSI C++ standard we have now.  The 
libstdc++ ABI had to make backwards-incompatible changes to meet 
the requirements of the finalized standard; most of those 
changes started with gcc-3.0 and ended with gcc-3.2.

For that reason, it's advantageous for a distro to provide at 
least the libstdc++ shared libraries from both gcc2 and gcc3, 
just to satisfy precompiled C++ apps.  Mixing gcc2-compiled C++ 
apps with gcc3-compiled C++ apps could work out 100% fine or 
could cause some pretty interesting problems.  Usually any 
problems it causes are not that destructive--just run-time link 
failures that prevent a specific app from running at all.

-- 
