On Wednesday 30 Apr 2003 9:25 am, Dr. David Kirkby wrote:
>1) The card has two VGA connectors on it - one black, one blue. Why?
>Only one (blue) seems to work.

I think that's for dual-head work. The card's a bit of a beast.

>2) When the machine is first powered up, it looks like there are a few
>initial messages to the screen which I can't get time to read,
>suggesting these appear before the monitor has chance to sync.
>Annoying to say the least, as perhaps these are important. Is this
>normal ??

Yup. Same on most pcs I know as well. The first bit is the video bios, and 
then you'll get something like ea e9 e8 e7 e6 e5....perfectly normal and 
nothing to bother about.

>4) The card remains in 640x480 at all times - even when Tru64 is
>running. This seems a bit odd.

You need to download and install the driver for it, and probably rebuild the 
kernel afterwards. See http://www.support.compaq.com/open3d/sw4d51t.htm.
Unfortunately there hasn't been (and probably never will be) linux drivers 
for these beasts. For linux use, Matrox Millenium II and some of the ATI 
radeon pci cards seem to be the best bet (see the archives of this list for a 
number of long discussions about this subject).

>6) It looks to me like some things (floppy contoller enbable/disable,
>perhaps cache) can only be accessed via the AlphaBIOS setup program.
>While I switched from AlphaBIOS to SRM in the AlphaBIOS setup program
>(bit like a nomal PC bios), how could I switch back to AlphaBIOS if I
>wanted to?

set os_type NT
init


cheers
john




