>newer Disks with a higher throughput e. g.  an U160 SCSI card remains.
>It's not only about Raid Cards...

I've used the software RAIDs with the standard Linux md raid tools for
striping (level 0) disks together with great success.  However, other
RAID levels, even level 1, requires some use of system resources.
Sometimes striped disks will run faster than externally attached
hardware RAID systems.  With the availability of high capacity, high
speed IDE drives, I could set up really cheap two drive stripes with
the onboard IDE controller and get really good performance for single
stream applications.  However, striping provides no redundancy, so a
single disk failure will take down the whole RAID.

There are a couple of cheap hardware RAIDs available on the market that
use IDE drives and SCSI host interfaces.  Often times, they provide lots
of protected (RAID 3 or 5) storage with very high throughput and they
are cheaper than buying the same capacity of SCSI drives.  That's the
direction we're headed.

eyc




